{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"How do I troubleshoot BGP connection issues over VPN?","context":"### Verify the number of routes that are advertised over the session\n\n**Important:** [100 is the maximum number of routes](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/s2svpn/vpn-limits.html#vpn-quotas-routes) that a BGP session supports. If the number of routes exceeds the quota, then the BGP's status changes from **Established** to **Idle**. For more information, see [Troubleshooting your customer gateway device](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/s2svpn/Troubleshooting.html).\n\nOn the customer gateway, verify that fewer than 100 routes are advertised over the BGP session. If the number of routes exceeds the quota, then take one of the following actions:\n\n* Advertise a default route to AWS.\n* Summarize the routes so that the number of routes received is less than 100.\n* [Migrate your VPN connection to a transit gateway](https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/transit-gateway-migrate-vpn). \n **Note:** A transit gateway supports up to 1,000 routes","url":"https://repost.aws/knowledge-center/troubleshoot-bgp-vpn"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS VPN | FAQs | Amazon Web Services (AWS)","context":"### What is the maximum number of routes that can be advertised to my VPN connection from my customer gateway device?\n\nYou can advertise a maximum of 100 routes to your Site-to-Site VPN connection on a virtual private gateway from your customer gateway device or a maximum of 1000 routes to your Site-to-Site VPN connection on an AWS Transit Gateway. For a VPN connection with Static routes, you will not be able to add more than 100 static routes. For a VPN connection with BGP, the BGP session will reset if you attempt to advertise more than the maximum for the gateway type","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/vpn/faqs/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN quotas","context":"## Routes\n\nAdvertised route sources include VPC routes, other VPN routes, and routes from Direct Connect\nvirtual interfaces. Advertised routes come from the route table that's associated with the\nVPN attachment.\n\n**Note:**\n\nIf you are using a virtual private gateway and route propagation is enabled on your VPC route table, both dynamic and static routes will automatically be added for your VPN connection, up to the limit of the VPC's route table. See Amazon VPC quotas in the *Amazon VPC User Guide* for further details.\n\n| Name | Default | Adjustable |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Dynamic routes advertised from a customer gateway device to a Site-to-Site VPN connection on a virtual private gateway | 100 | No |\n| Routes advertised from a Site-to-Site VPN connection on a virtual private gateway to a customer gateway device | 1,000 | No |\n| Dynamic routes advertised from a customer gateway device to a Site-to-Site VPN connection on a transit gateway | 1,000 | No |\n| Routes advertised from a Site-to-Site VPN connection on a transit gateway to a customer gateway device | 5,000 | No |\n| Static routes from a customer gateway device to a Site-to-Site VPN connection on a virtual private gateway | 100 | No |","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/vpn/latest/s2svpn/vpn-limits.html"}]}}

